Enquiries: Tel: (677) 23951/21427, Email: STATS –Social@mof.gov.sb

                   
i 
 
 
Preface 
 
This report provides basic table information to the analytical report (‘2019 National Population and Housing 
Census National Report (Volume 1 ) of the 2019 Population and Housing Census of Solomon Islands. The data 
presented is referenced to the census date of the midnight of the 24th of November 2019. It is anticipated that the 
extended statistical information contained in this report will further inform, not only the wider community, but 
decision-makers in various capacities in the Solomon Islands. 
 
The report is presented in two parts. Part 1, presents the statistical tables (person tables  and household tables) 
itemizing the data provided in the analytical report. Part 2 provides a summary of the operations of the 2019 
Census and Part 3 contains the appendices. It is noted that additional information on the population projections 
will be provided separately through an excel file that will be uploaded on the SINSO website. 
 
While the set of tables presented here adheres to the United Nations Principles and Recommendations for 
Population and Housing Censuses, and where there is continuity in m aintaining formatting standards of 
comparability with the previous censuses, caution should be considered in any direct or explicit comparisons on 
specific variables amongst past censuses noting certain changes in operations, definitions, and methods over time.  
 
Although there may have been slight changes to the numbering and ordering of the tables corresponding to each 
subject matter grouping, as well as situations where the titles may have changed due to the structure and the 
formatting specifications, it is important to note that the table numberings relating to the categorizing of persons 
and households correspond to the respective chapters in the volume 1 report. 
 
The tireless efforts of a number of people and agencies towards the successful implementation of the 2019 Census 
enumeration and the final release of the key findings through the analytical report and this report are 
acknowledged. Firstly, the government of Solomon Islands is acknowledged through the Minister of Finance and 
Treasury (MOFT), Honorable Harry Kuma for his leadership and support in terms of funding for the 2019 Census 
operation, and Mr McKinnie Dentana, Permanent Secretary for MOFT for his leadership and management 
oversight of the census operations at the ministry level.  
 
Dr.Michael Levins and Jomer Manongsong are commended for technical advice on data processing and training, 
and Mr. Joshua Neale, Data Analyst under the New Zealand Volunteer (VSA) Programme for his assistance in 
data processing and data analysis. Mr. Dougl as Kimi is also credited for his leadership and chairmanship of the 
Census Users Committee that comprised of members of key government ministries who contributed in the design 
of the questionnaire. Moreover, special appreciation is conveyed to Dr. Willie Lahari, MOFT-SINSO Technical 
Adviser for his technical assistance and capacity building in the planning and design, and implementation of the 
2019 census enumeration, and the analysis of the data. 
 
Sincere thanks also go to the following donors by share of grant funding support: to our Solomon Island 
Government (SIG) for being the main provider of funding support for the 2019 Census Project with grant 
financing (93.2%); Republic of China (ROC-Taiwan (4.9%); Central Bank of Solomon Islands (0.2%); R-Wash  
(1.2%), Ministry of Health and Medical Services; and UNICEF,(0.5%), Their financial assistance has 
tremendously supported the activities of the 2019 Census Project of the Solomon Island.  Details of the 2019 
Census budget is provided in Appendix 2. 
 
 
                   
ii 
 
Further words of commendations are conveyed to the Census Provincial Coordinators namely, Loyce Pabulu, 
Elleni Taisia, Anna Pitaboe, Clera Sao’hu, Alfred Palo, Josephat Tako, Desmond Papage, Joy Talo Mazini, 
Anterlyn Tuzakana, Reginald Mara, Hilda Diudi, a nd Michael Dimola, Late Raphael Aipaina and NSDS Staff 
are also thanked for their valuable contributions rendered to the success of 2019 Census.  
 
The Solomon Island National Statistics Office (SINSO) and Census Management further expresses its 
appreciation to all the Census Project staff and the rest of the staff of the SINSO including Michael Dimola for 
his leadership in census logistics and management, Samson Kanamoli for support in GIS/Mapping and oversight 
over data processing, and Reginald Mara for support provided in data processing. The support from the Provincial 
Governments during the census enumeration is also appreciated including all the hard work undertaken by the 
Census District Supervisors, Chief Area Managers, Area Managers, Supervisors and Enumerators and Trainers. 
 
The SINSO is also grateful to the following media outlets: SINSO Media, Solomon Islands Broadcasting 
Cooperation (SIBC), Solomon Star Newspaper and Island Sun Newspaper for the publicity programs since the 
initial stage of conducting 2019 Census.  
  
Finally, let me salute the people of Solomon Islands including families, households and expatriate residents who 
participated in the 2019 Census enumeration, for availing their time to answer the questions asked and in 
cooperating with the enumerators during interviews. Your contributions have contributed to the success of the 
2019 Population and Housing Census for the Solomon Islands. 
  
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
Douglas Kimi 
Census Commissioner and Government Statistician 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
                   
